A||N||8||SandyKnip||Alternator belt problem||||5||
Z||000000||SandyKnip||06-27-2000||11:20 PM||||Just replaced the alternator (again) in my 86 SE V6.  It had been about a year since the last time.  I have a continuing problem with belts.  They either squeal, or if tightened up enough to stop, they break.  I'm tired of sitting by the side of the road.  Do they make an extra-strong belt, or any other suggestions?  I also have a similar problem with the AC belt.  Either it squeals or it breaks.  No happy medium.  My hubby is the mechanic and is very experienced, so that's not the problem.  Thanks,  <BR>Sandy K. ||||reg||5
Z||000001||dogtired||06-28-2000||06:20 AM||||SandyKnip<BR>Not sure which motor you have? On my V6s, I had quite a time getting them tight enough till I purchased a belt tightening tool from JC Whitney.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I always do it through the wheelwell. I think Gates makes a heavy duty belt? Not sure, I may be thinking commerical??  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"> Make sure when you buy belts to get a superior quality like Gates. Not too long ago I went to a parts house and ask for a set of belts and they handed me some cheep junk, I think made in XXXXX ?? None the less, I passed and went down the road.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  Good luck.||||reg||6
Z||000002||rogergarrison||06-28-2000||11:47 PM||||check to see if all the pullys/components are lined up properly. Ive seen things like alternator off 1/2 inch or more from running true. also might look to see if there are any breaks or burrs on any pullys that may be cutting belt over time||||reg||1
Z||000003||StuGood||06-28-2000||11:50 PM||||Welcome  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">!<P>While this may seem too obvious to even mention, you'll not have much luck with an oily belt.  If there's significant oil leakage onto the belt, it won't stay tight, and won't last long.<P>Alternative: Go see Cal Worthington, and borrow his rope to tie around those pulleys  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> !    ||||reg||1
Z||000004||SandyKnip||06-29-2000||02:45 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by StuGood:<BR><B>Welcome   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">!<P>While this may seem too obvious to even mention, you'll not have much luck with an oily belt.  If there's significant oil leakage onto the belt, it won't stay tight, and won't last long.<P>Alternative: Go see Cal Worthington, and borrow his rope to tie around those pulleys   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> !    </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well, Stu, that is a bit obvious.  My car doesn't have any oil leak or oil burning problems.  If I could keep the stinking belts on it, it would be almost perfect.  Hubby is checking for burrs and alignment, etc. and is going to try belt dressing.  How does a guy from Kansas know about Cal Worthington?  <P>Sandy||||reg||7
Z||000005||StuGood||06-29-2000||05:51 PM||||He has lived in Seattle off and on over the years, that's how  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.  <P>If it makes you feel better, there have been tons of belt-tightening questions posted, so you're not alone.  Try doing a SEARCH through the archives (search for "belt" in the subject line), and you'll get more tips than you can shake a pry bar at.  <P>It IS possible to do it right - I have had quiet, trouble-free belts on my Fieros for many years.<P>Good luck  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> !       ||||reg||12
Z||000006||SandyKnip||06-30-2000||02:20 AM||||Thanks a bunch!  We'll keep trying.  ||||reg||6
Z||000007||theogre||06-30-2000||12:56 PM||||what brand of belts are you using. Many places only carry Dayco, and frankly Dayco product bites. Other cheapo brands also stink. Try using Gates and see if that works any bettter. I've never had a problem with a Gates belt. (I have to mail order them for mine. Carparts.com has them.)<P>I'll assume you all checked the bearings in the alternator AC and water pump. Did you also inspect the pulleys for wear? This kind of problem is common if the sides of the pulleys are worn allot. It can be dificult to tell. If a previous owner left the belts squalling allot then the crank pulley may be too worn for the belt to bite properly. You'll need another crank pulley if that's the case.||||reg||1
Z||000008||SandyKnip||06-30-2000||04:48 PM||||I don't know if the bearings have been checked in the AC and water pump.  The alternator is new.  I doubt if the pulleys are bad -- the car only had 20,000 on it when we acquired it, and I've put another 32,000 on it.  I'm guessing it's the brand of belts.  I'll look into the mail order source.  Thanks!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><BR>||||reg||6
